    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is understandable to look for patterns when you miss out on a role you are well-qualified for, but **the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women is not, on its own, evidence of bias or a sign of an unfair process.**\n\nHere is an objective breakdown of why, along with how best to approach the situation:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition\n* **Role-based appointments:** Selection panels at public broadcasters are typically composed of specific post-holders (e.g., Head of News, Senior HR Partner, Head of Digital, External Assessor). If the individuals holding those specific roles at the time happen to be women, the panel reflects the leadership structure rather than an intent to influence the outcome based on gender.\n* **Perception vs. Evidence:** While organizations often aim for demographic balance on interview panels, an all-female panel—much like historically all-male panels—frequently occurs due to scheduling, role requirements, or organizational demographics. Without explicit remarks, procedural breaches, or demonstrable discrimination, the gender makeup alone does not indicate bias.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Differing Qualifications and Strategic Fit\n* **Tenure vs. Skill Set:** While your 15 years in news and 5 years as deputy editor represent significant leadership experience, years on the job are rarely the sole deciding factor.\n* **Digital Strategy:** The announcement highlighted that the successful candidate had **\"strong experience of digital media.\"** Most public broadcasters are undergoing aggressive digital transformations, shifting resources and strategy from traditional linear broadcasts to streaming, mobile, and digital-first journalism. A candidate with fewer overall years but deep, specialized experience in digital transformation may have matched their strategic priorities more closely.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Risks of Raising the Panel's Gender\n* **Professional Reputation:** Making an issue of the panel’s gender without concrete evidence of discrimination is likely to be viewed as bad faith or resentment. In the relatively small world of public broadcasting, this could damage working relationships and future career prospects.\n* **Legal / HR Standard:** From an employment perspective, pointing solely to the gender of the panel does not establish a prima facie case of unlawful discrimination.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Why You Haven't Heard Back Yet\n* **HR Bureaucracy:** Two weeks is a very standard delay in public sector organizations. Drafting formal written interview feedback often requires approval from multiple panel members and HR, which routinely takes several weeks.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up politely on feedback:**  \n   Send a constructive follow-up note to HR or the hiring manager. Frame your request around professional development:\n   > *\"I wanted to follow up on my request for written feedback regarding the News Editor interview. As I am keen to continue developing my skills—particularly in strategic areas like digital journalism—any guidance the panel can provide on where I can strengthen my profile for future senior editorial roles would be greatly appreciated.\"*\n\n2. **Evaluate the feedback objectively:**  \n   When the feedback arrives, focus on specific areas they identified (e.g., vision for digital news, interview presentation, leadership style).\n\n3. **Check formal guidelines (if applicable):**  \n   If you believe there were genuine procedural irregularities (e.g., questions outside the job specification, failure to follow public broadcaster appointment codes), consult your union representative or HR policy guidelines for the formal review process, keeping the focus entirely on policy and merit.\n",
